C = [
("h1", "2. The Word “Sign” Before the Miracles"),
("p", "Claim: a sign in Scripture is an act, an object or a person that points to something the LORD has said or will do, and many signs are not miracles. The word is used of the lights in the sky, the bow in the cloud, a baby in a manger and a prophet lying on his side before a model of a city. The miracle-sign is one use of the word among several, and the volume reads the other uses first so that the miracle-sign is read in its place."),
("sub", "2.1 Signs that are not wonders"),
("p", "The first use is in creation: “And God said, Let there be lights in the firmament of the heaven to divide the day from the night; and let them be for signs, and for seasons, and for days, and years” (Genesis 1:14). The lights are for signs, and the text lists the signs beside seasons, days and years. The lights do not do a wonder; they mark time and place for those who look."),
("p", "After the flood the LORD fixes a sign to a promise: “And God said, This is the token of the covenant which I make between me and you and every living creature that is with you, for perpetual generations: I do set my bow in the cloud, and it shall be for a token of a covenant between me and the earth” (Genesis 9:12, 13). The KJV uses “token” where it elsewhere uses “sign,” and the object is a covenant sign: it points to a promise already made, and it is given to the party the promise concerns. Abraham receives circumcision in the same way: “and it shall be a token of the covenant betwixt me and you” (Genesis 17:11). At the Passover the blood is “for a token upon the houses where ye are” (Exodus 12:13), and the LORD says of it, “when I see the blood, I will pass over you.”"),
("p", "Three marks recur. The sign is given by the LORD. It is given to a named party. And it stands for a statement the LORD has already made. The bow, the flesh and the blood do not authenticate a messenger; they hold a promise before the eyes of the people who received it."),
("sub", "2.2 Signs set in time, signs set in persons"),
("p", "A second use sets the sign in the future, as a pledge. When Moses asks, “Who am I, that I should go unto Pharaoh?” the LORD answers, “Certainly I will be with thee; and this shall be a token unto thee, that I have sent thee: When thou hast brought forth the people out of Egypt, ye shall serve God upon this mountain” (Exodus 3:11, 12). The sign is an event that has not yet happened. Moses is given it so that he may go on the word, and the confirmation arrives after the obedience."),
("p", "A third use sets the sign in persons. Isaiah says, “Behold, I and the children whom the LORD hath given me are for signs and for wonders in Israel from the LORD of hosts, which dwelleth in mount Zion” (Isaiah 8:18). The prophet and his sons are the sign: their names and their presence carry the message. Ezekiel is told to lay siege to a model of the city and the LORD says, “This shall be a sign to the house of Israel” (Ezekiel 4:3). Later he is told, “Say, I am your sign: like as I have done, so shall it be done unto them: they shall remove and go into captivity” (Ezekiel 12:11). In each of these the sign is an act the prophet performs, and the people to whom it is given are named: Israel."),
("p", "The announcement to the shepherds belongs in the same group. The angel says, “And this shall be a sign unto you; Ye shall find the babe wrapped in swaddling clothes, lying in a manger” (Luke 2:12). The sign is a fact the shepherds can check, and it is given so that the word they have heard may be known to be the word of the LORD. It is addressed to the shepherds at Bethlehem."),
("sub", "2.3 The sign between the LORD and Israel"),
("p", "One sign is named in the plainest terms of audience: “Speak thou also unto the children of Israel, saying, Verily my sabbaths ye shall keep: for it is a sign between me and you throughout your generations; that ye may know that I am the LORD that doth sanctify you” (Exodus 31:13). The sign is “between me and you,” the “you” is “the children of Israel,” and the stated purpose is knowledge: “that ye may know that I am the LORD.” The passage does not call the sabbath a miracle. It calls the sabbath a sign, and it names the two parties. Study 78 reads the sabbath passages in full, and this volume takes from Exodus 31:13 only the pattern: a sign is a statement of relation, with a giver, a hearer and a stated purpose."),
("sub", "2.4 What the other uses leave for the miracle-sign"),
("p", "The miracle-signs that follow in the volume fit the same shape. The rod that becomes a serpent has a giver, the LORD. It has a hearer, the elders of Israel. And it has a stated purpose, “that they may believe” (Exodus 4:5). The miracle-sign adds one thing to the other uses: the sign is a visible act of power, and the act is the guarantee that the messenger speaks for the LORD. The other signs hold a promise; the miracle-sign holds a messenger."),
("p", "The distinction bears on every later question. If the word “sign” named only miracles, the question of whether miracles continue would settle the question of whether signs continue. The word names more, and the passages that apply it to miracles apply it to messengers and to the people the messengers address. The volume therefore asks of any claim about signs what the passage asks: who is the messenger, and who is the hearer."),
("ctbl", "Signs that are not miracles, and what each holds.", [
    ("Passage", "What is called a sign or token", "To whom and for what"),
    ("Genesis 1:14", "The lights in the firmament", "For signs, seasons, days and years"),
    ("Genesis 9:12, 13", "The bow in the cloud", "The covenant with the earth and every living creature"),
    ("Genesis 17:11", "Circumcision", "A token of the covenant between God and Abraham"),
    ("Exodus 12:13", "The blood on the houses", "Israel, for the passing over"),
    ("Exodus 31:13", "The sabbaths", "A sign between the LORD and the children of Israel"),
    ("Isaiah 8:18", "The prophet and his children", "Signs and wonders in Israel"),
    ("Ezekiel 4:3; 12:11", "The prophet’s acts", "The house of Israel"),
    ("Luke 2:12", "The babe in the manger", "The shepherds"),
  ], [0.22, 0.38, 0.40]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The word “sign” names a pointer given by the LORD to a named party, and not only a miracle.",
    "Covenant signs hold a promise; miracle-signs hold a messenger.",
    "Every passage states a giver, a hearer and a purpose, and the volume reads the miracle-signs by the same three.",
    "The question of continuation cannot be settled by the word alone, because the word names more than miracles.",
  ]),
]
